# V. Proof completeness and hidden assumptions

## The frame and pairing architecture survives dependent formulas

The scalar ring is the Boolean formula algebra modulo logical equivalence, with XOR as addition and conjunction as multiplication. Thus the identities are equalities of equivalence classes, not claims that two strings of syntax are identical. Formula dependence is permitted: $X$ and $Y$ may share variables, coincide, be complementary, or be constants.

For the displayed frame $\mathsf S_X$, the diagonal of $\mathsf S_X^2$ is $X\Updownarrow\neg X=1$, and each off-diagonal entry is the XOR of two copies of $X\land\neg X=0$. Hence the frame is involutory. The upper-left entry of $\mathsf S_X[\Theta]\mathsf S_Y$ is the four-term disjoint-minterm expansion of $X\Theta Y$; the other entries change the appropriate polarities. This proves Theorem 3.1 for arbitrary formulas. Multiplication by the two inverse frames recovers $[\Theta]$ without a valuation or independence assumption.

This distinction matters for $X=P$, $Y=\neg P$. There is no valuation making both true, but the normal form and inverse are still valid. The current manuscript correctly states this. A criticism that such dependence destroys the LM architecture is therefore wrong for this source.

The pairing theorem follows from

$$
\langle A|\mathsf S_X=\langle A\Leftrightarrow X|,
\qquad
\mathsf S_Y|B\rangle=|Y\Leftrightarrow B\rangle.
$$

The first component of the left identity is precisely the disjoint sum of the two agreement cases; the second component is its complement. One-hot selection, interpreted in the formula algebra, then gives the stated connective of the two agreement bits. The result is a relationship formula. It does not claim that pairing is a probability, an expectation, or a test that both agreements separately hold. For implication, a false antecedent agreement can make the paired result true.

There is a useful consequence worth emphasizing more strongly: **the entire LM still encodes the connective after dependent substitution**. For example, when $X=Y=P$, the reference formula $P\Updownarrow P$ is zero, but its full polarity LM is the swap matrix, not the zero LM. Invertible frame reconstruction distinguishes the two. This is a useful explanatory corollary of the normal form, not an additional historical novelty claim.

## Why the selector theorem proves the full outer-operation law

Write $q_w(T)=\mathop{\Updownarrow}_i w_iT_i$. Linearity gives XOR preservation. Orthogonality gives

$$
q_w(T)q_w(U)
=\mathop{\Updownarrow}_{i,j}w_iw_jT_iU_j
=\mathop{\Updownarrow}_{i}w_iT_iU_i
=q_w(TU).
$$

The weights sum to one, so $q_w(1)=1$; complement follows. XOR, conjunction and the unit generate every Boolean term operation. Conversely, for the coordinate idempotents $e_i$, preservation of $e_ie_j=0$ forces $w_iw_j=0$, and preservation of the unit forces their sum to one. The stated converse is properly restricted to the displayed formula-linear maps. It is not a classification of arbitrary Boolean-algebra homomorphisms without that qualification.

For logical pairing, weights $A^{\langle r\rangle}B^{\langle s\rangle}$ are orthogonal even when some are identically zero. This is why dependence of $A$ and $B$ does not break coherence. An arbitrary contraction with nonorthogonal weights does not enjoy the same property; weights $(1,1)$ already fail unitality under XOR.

## The arbitrary-arity statements have general proofs

At an assignment $x$, the value of $X_i^{\langle\alpha_i\rangle}$ is $x_i\Updownarrow\alpha_i\Updownarrow1$. Therefore

$$
\mathcal M_f(x,\alpha)=f(x\Updownarrow\alpha\Updownarrow\mathbf1).
$$

This single identity verifies the indexing convention, valuation formula, orbit structure and the pointwise superposition law for every finite arity. Setting $x=\mathbf1$ recovers the truth tensor in the free-reference algebra. Applying a polarity substitution to the reference variables is equivalent to changing the same index mask. Conversely, an equivariant tensor is recovered from its all-positive cell. These are full arguments for arbitrary $n$, not inferences from the arity-three tests.

For higher pairing, at any valuation the product of the $A_i$-minterms selects exactly the polarity tuple $\alpha=(v(A_i))_i$. Substitution into the preceding identity gives

$$
f\bigl(v(A_1)\Updownarrow v(X_1)\Updownarrow1,\ldots,
       v(A_n)\Updownarrow v(X_n)\Updownarrow1\bigr),
$$

which is $f$ on the agreement bits. Since the equality holds for every propositional valuation, the formulas are equivalent. Inserting this short argument would make Theorem 6.15's proof more transparent than its current compressed reference to the selector theorem.

The intrinsic equivariance characterization has a narrower scope than the binary normal form: it is stated in $\mathcal F_{\mathbf X}$ with free reference generators. An independent negation of one formal generator need not descend to a quotient imposing, for example, $X_1=X_2$. Do not export that group-action statement to constrained references without specifying the induced action. This is a scope boundary, not a missing hypothesis in the current theorem.

## An exact symbolic signed-frame contract should be added

The numeric right action is correct as written. What deserves an explicit statement is how the symbolic references and polarity indices transform together. Let $P_\sigma z=(z_{\sigma(i)})_i$ and $\phi(z)=P_\sigma z\Updownarrow\epsilon$. The correct identity is

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal L_{\mathbf X}(f\circ\phi)[\alpha]
=\mathcal L_{P_\sigma\mathbf X}(f)
      [P_\sigma\alpha\Updownarrow\epsilon]
=\mathcal L_{\phi(\mathbf X)}(f)[P_\sigma\alpha].
}
$$

All three expressions have value $f(P_\sigma x\Updownarrow P_\sigma\alpha\Updownarrow\epsilon\Updownarrow\mathbf1)$. A polarity mask may be placed in the references or the slot indices, but not independently in both. Applying it twice cancels it. For one variable, $f$ the identity, $x=\alpha=1$ and $\epsilon=1$, the desired transformed value is zero; negating both the reference and the index incorrectly returns one.

This is a proposed clarification of Theorem 6.12(3) and Section 9, **not a refutation of their common-reindexing identity**. It is especially important if the article is sold as a formal specification. Likewise, a claim that a scalar pairing is invariant under changing coordinates must transport the selector states too; the current preservation-of-outer-operation theorem does not assert invariance under a fixed, untransported selector.

## Matrix multiplication, block construction and rank

Matched intermediate references cancel because $\mathsf S_Y^2=I$. With unmatched references, a residual $\mathsf S_Y\mathsf S_W$ generally remains. The resulting kernel sums mod-two contributions over the intermediate Boolean index. It is not ordinary existential relational composition and is not pointwise conjunction. The current paper distinguishes these correctly.

The flattening proof is valid for any ordered bipartition, including noncontiguous indices. The dimensions are $2^p\times2^q$ and the two one-hot tensor states have matching lengths. When $p=q$, identifying both coordinate spaces with $\mathbb F_2^{2^p}$ supports the endomorphism wording; the semantic row and column labels should nevertheless remain visible. If empty blocks are allowed, add the conventional empty tensor/state of length one.

The block-lift theorem is conditional. It does not say every function has the form $g(a)\Theta h(b)$ with one Boolean output from each block. A two-bit equality relation $f(a,b)=1$ exactly when $a=b$ has four distinct rows; a one-bit $g(a)$ can distinguish at most two row patterns. That relation is a useful limitation example.

For rank, every conjunctive term gives an outer product and thus rank at most one. A sum of $k$ such matrices has rank at most $k$. A rank factorization gives exactly $r$ outer products, so the minimum is $r$. Nonzero rank-one factors are unique over $\mathbb F_2$ because there is only one nonzero scalar. Zero factors are not unique, and $k=0$ should be admitted through the empty XOR. The invariant depends on the bipartition: $(X_1\Leftrightarrow X_2)\land(X_3\Leftrightarrow X_4)$ has rank one across $12\mid34$ and rank four across $13\mid24$.

## Other proof and notation details

The ANF parity statement follows directly from Boolean Mobius inversion; the current $n\ge1$ condition is correct. The rotation matrix and its associated input transformation agree entry by entry. The spectral statements are correct for XOR--AND field arithmetic: OR has no eigenvalue in $\mathbb F_2$ and XOR has a repeated eigenvalue one with a one-dimensional eigenspace. The four truth outputs are not the two-dimensional CM spectrum.

Two local corrections are advisable. First, the STP comparison uses $t$ as a row while the later $\operatorname{vec}$ convention is a column. Define $t=\operatorname{vec}([\Theta])^T$ there. Second, the phrase restricting formula-matrix multiplication to "this subsection only" is misleading because the same convention is used again in the matched-frame theorem. Neither correction changes a mathematical conclusion.

The closure proposition is scalar closure. It does not mean that arbitrary vector addition preserves the special formula-state family: $|A\rangle\Updownarrow|A\rangle=0$ is not of the form $(B,\neg B)^T$. The present text does not assert that stronger closure, but a one-sentence warning would prevent an easy misreading.

