# XI. Scope and cross-paper boundary analysis

## Foundations versus compiler

The actual companion compiler manuscript contains the binary formula LM, its valuation, logical pairing and valuation/pairing commutation in its LM section. These are genuine overlapping mathematical passages, not overlap inferred solely from an ownership register. Its implementation discussion also makes clear that the compiler operates on numeric four-bit CM tokens and pair metadata, not on arbitrary symbolic formula-valued matrices. Therefore the foundations calculus should not be credited with an implemented formula-level compiler that the companion does not actually implement.

This overlap is manageable. The foundations article should own the full definitions and proofs. The compiler can retain a short, self-contained statement of the numeric frame/fusion invariant, with a concise explanation of its relation to the LM viewpoint and a precise cross-reference. It should not be necessary to read a performance paper to understand Theorem 4.1 here, nor to reproduce every foundational theorem in the compiler.

The companion's unfinished confirmatory experiment and the old hybrid/fallback review issues are **not mathematical defects in this foundations manuscript**. No speed or memory claim is used as a premise here. Conversely, the correctness of this calculus is not evidence of compiler profitability. The report does not certify the companion's source, experimental protocol or performance.

## Foundations versus phase and modal work

The archive's correctness/model-boundary report explicitly separates native two-by-two CM logic, a rotation-generated coefficient algebra, shared modules, literal tensor systems and additional allowed gates/measurements. Selected actual source passages were inspected, not just the ownership summaries. Those distinctions reinforce the current foundations boundary: truth-table selection alone does not establish Bell phenomena, teleportation, interference resources or a physical theory.

The current manuscript correctly identifies literal rotation as a signed coordinate action and treats cyclic convolution as an additional multiplication. Its short group-algebra isomorphism is mathematically sound, but the development of units, nilpotents, operational restrictions and modal resources belongs elsewhere. Nothing found in those companion corrections invalidates the formula frame or agreement pairing. No modal correctness campaign was rerun as part of this foundations audit.

The spectral appendix can remain as a defensive clarification, but it should not grow into another paper inside this one. A compact explanation that matrix elements and eigenvalues are different semantics, with OR as a counterexample and the diagonal truth representation as a comparison, is enough for most readers.

## Structural computation and decomposition

The structural-computation collection describes research endpoints and incomplete empirical work, not a completed experiment supporting the present foundations. The ordinary rank/separability theorem belongs naturally here as a mathematical explanation of flattenings. Claims about avoiding materialization, structural retrieval, persistent decomposition or end-to-end speed belong to the structural/computational work and require their own evidence.

**Standalone assessment:** the current foundations mathematics is substantially self-contained. The remaining cross-paper task is editorial ownership and reduction of duplicated proofs, not recovery of an external theorem without which this paper fails.

# XII. Completeness audit

The main mathematical objects, scalar algebra, valuation map, polarity convention, ordered flattening and pointwise operation are defined. The proofs needed for the principal claims are present. The theorem count does not conceal a missing central result. The following changes would make the paper more complete as a standalone article without enlarging its research scope.

First, make the symbolic signed-frame action explicit using the identity in Section V of this report. This closes the gap between an informal "typed" promise and an executable convention. Second, explain the higher-pairing selector step rather than relying on an abrupt appeal to a general theorem. Third, state the zero/empty-sum and optional empty-block conventions where relevant.

Fourth, supply one complete worked example from a logical expression through two differently framed occurrences, alignment, operator combination, symbolic pairing and numeric valuation. The current short examples each illustrate a correct local identity, but the reader does not yet see the whole promised architecture doing one job. A small noncontiguous rectangular example would also help readers use the assembly definition.

Fifth, publish the verification source actually supporting the new revision and accurately state what was checked. The present verification appendix is incomplete as an artifact claim, despite the finite mathematics being independently sound.

Finally, align headings with content. Appendix A is titled a compact-CM atlas, but presents representative spectral examples and the idempotent classification rather than a full sixteen-row atlas. Either rename it to "Spectral boundary and examples" or supply a genuine complete table as supplementary material. The independent atlas JSON in this audit can support the latter after integration and review.

The binary LM basis appendix is useful but could be shortened by referring to the normal form. The compatibility appendix largely repeats the main theorem and could become a one-page reference box. The notation table is valuable as an appendix. The finite verification note should stay separate from the proofs, with its executable supplement clearly linked. No full clone formalism, operator-valued enrichment, new phase theory, or physical measurement axioms are needed to make the present article complete.

# XIII. Exposition and editorial review

## Organize around the useful mechanism

The reader should encounter a practical semantic problem before a long list of representation levels. A good opening is the reversed-implication example: two local operators have identical numeric arrays, but canceling them without their operand frames gives the wrong answer. This motivates labelled frames. The formula frame then explains how to transport them; the selector theorem explains why pairing and outer operations agree.

The strongest short narrative is: **truth kernel; symbolic frame; agreement pairing; Boolean-homomorphism coherence; typed higher-arity extension**. Ordinary rank and rotation boundaries are applications/reference material, not competing main themes.

The introduction, abstract, status paragraph, related-work section, discussion and conclusion repeatedly explain that the individual ingredients are classical and that no physics or speedup is assumed. Those cautions are appropriate, but repetition makes the article sound defensive and interrupts the mathematics. Give one precise contribution statement, one operation/type table and one boundary paragraph; use short cross-references thereafter. Removing repeated disclaimers is not permission to strengthen the novelty claims.

